      "message": "nit: Some part of the code makes use of the `?` operator to simplify early return pattern which contains repetitive `match` and `Some(x) \u003d\u003e x`. I find using `?` is more concise and readable:\n```\n        match (|| {\n            let addr \u003d RawAddress::from_string(address.clone()).ok_or(\"Can\u0027t start SCO\")?;\n            info!(\"Start sco call for {}\", address);\n            let hfp \u003d self.hfp.as_mut().ok_or(\"Uninitialized HFP\")?;\n\n            if hfp.connect_audio(addr, sco_offload, force_cvsd) !\u003d 0 {\n                return Err(\"SCO connect_audio status failed.\");\n            }\n\n            info!(\"SCO connect_audio status success.\");\n            Ok(())\n        })() {\n            Ok(_) \u003d\u003e true,\n            Err(msg) \u003d\u003e {\n                warn!(\"{}\", msg);\n                false\n            }\n        }\n```",
